00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:11.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this week the government has been  celebrating its first bespoke  post-brexit trade deal  the arrangement of australia is seen as  a coup for global britain  but it wasn't long before detractors  criticized that it would put british  farming under unfair competition  so should brits be welcoming the deal  i'm joined now by tony abbott  advisor to the uk board of trade and the  former australian prime minister  as well as james forsyth the spectator's  political editor  tony you've been pushing for this deal  for over a year so  did you celebrate when the news came  through absolutely  like just about every other australian i  see this deal as restoring a  relationship that should never have been  broken back in the 1970s  when britain joined what was to become  the european union  britain and australia have always been  family  and always should be family and this  deal is a way of re-establishing  the kinds of family bonds that we always  had  and will now have again james from the  uk perspective  uh how big a deal is this because we've  heard a lot about how oh  well it actually won't have a huge  impact on the economy in terms of growth  but  is it more a political triumph i think  there are three big  implications of this deal the first is  if the uk couldn't have signed a free  trade deal for australia  a country which is also governed  english-speaking country governed by a  center-right government  it would have been very hard to see who  on earth the uk was going to sign a  trade deal with  i so that's the first significance the  second is i think this puts the uk  on course to join the comprehensive and  progressive trans-pacific  partnership big trading blocks centered  around  the pacific ocean with and i think the  particular significance of that is  if the wto uh becomes uh fundamentally  unworkable because of tensions between  china and the west  i think groups like cptp are going to be  what countries fall back on  and then i think the third significance  of it is  that i've always thought the domestic  politics of a u.s uk trade deal are very  difficult  but i think after the midterm elections  you might see the biden administration  look at taking the you  the us into cptp and so i think in a way  this perhaps  paves the way for that deal as well  tony when we're looking at the pros and  cons of this deal  one of the points of controversy here in  the uk and the build up uh has been on  agri food on australian farmers uh  there's been some kickback  uh in the end a deal has been agreed but  there is a 15-year wait  um at times were you worried the  protectionists might stop that  in the uk look uh  i was certainly a little dismayed at the  level of protectionism  in some corners of britain but  look every sectional interest is  effectively a vested interest and  vested interests will always put their  pitch but in the end i was confident  that the good sense of the british  people and the good sense of the boris  johnson  government would prevail the important  thing to remember  is that this deal is better for both  countries  from day one from day one  australian goods and australians will  have better access to britain  and british goods and britons will have  better access  to australia tony is there anything in  the deal that you think  australia would have been reluctant the  government to sign up to because because  there are various uh you know  commitments on climate change at the uk  side pushed for and appeared to be in it  katie look um obviously we have yet to  see  uh how the thing has elaborated in  writing  but my understanding is that the uk  government and the australian government  have agreed  uh that further commitments if any on  emissions reduction will be pursued as  part of the cop process  now as you know i'm not an emission  zealot  i would like to see emissions restrained  and if possible reduced but i certainly  don't want to see  economies turned upside down and  impoverished in order to do so  so i certainly think that there's a lot  of overblown rhetoric when it comes to  climate  that said i am a conservationist  i want to see the highest possible  environmental standards the highest  possible  animal welfare standards the highest  possible labor standards  and i think any britain that knows  australia would realize that we are all  conservationists that hardy  we certainly don't mistreat our animals  and  australian workers get a very a fair go  indeed  as indeed should be the case in britain  you see it should be very easy  to do a deal between two countries that  have such  comparable attitudes and such comparable  living standards  and that that's why it was very  important to get this deal done  and why it's very important that it was  such a  comprehensive and high quality deal so  um after waiting through a bit of  unnecessary rgb  behind closed doors that seems to be  where  thanks to the good work of dan t and the  australian minister and  liz trust the british minister and of  course our respective prime ministers  scott morrison and boris johnson that's  where we've got to  and james i suppose just quickly a final  question or two do you think that  climate change  could be a point of uh i suppose  conflict between the australian  government and the uk government because  we know the two sides have a lot in  common  and but boris johnson is pushing very  hard on his net zero on cop 26 we're  just in that g7  so is that going to be something to  watch out for  look i think if you want to phase out  coal australia is  is one of the countries you need to  persuade of  the merits of that uh i think that you  know this is going to be a major part of  uk diplomacy and i use you saw at the  the g7 summit in cornwall you know that  the importance  that boris johnson placed on and also  the fact that you know the uk's  integrated review describes this as one  of  uh one of if not the uk's foreign policy  priorities  i i think the interesting question is  you know to how much you to what extent  there are kind of coalitions of the  willing on this  uh and to what extent that you do  everything through  uh the the the um process  look if i could just katie if i could  just make a point here  um china is by far the world's biggest  emitter  uh china produces something like 30  percent of global emissions  china is not promising to reduce its  emissions  at all over the next decade it's  promised  to get to ned zero by 2060  10 years after britain and other  countries and to be honest  china's promises are not reliable  china's emissions have gone up by almost  a hundred percent  uh over the last 15 years china is  absolutely relentlessly dedicated to  making itself stronger  vis-a-vis the west and and we need to be  extremely  careful about doing things that are  going to make us weaker  vis-a-vis china and look we all want to  reduce emissions  but to be absolutely blunt with you  there are far more important  geopolitical questions  at issue today and the last thing that  any of us should be doing  is making ourselves economically weaker  because in the end military strength is  a function of economic strength is  making ourselves  economically weaker in ways which  are not going to materially impact  on climate because of the massive  increase in emissions out of china  james the uk government boris johnson  has been talking about china with scott  morrison on his visit here  so do you think that uh boris johnson um  his view reflects a little bit of what  tony's been saying or not when it comes  to that approach and that balancing act  i think there is a china aspect to this  trade deal if you look at ever since  australia called for an independent who  investigation into the origins of  coronavirus  china has been trying to use its  economic relationship with australia  to put pressure on canberra you know  you've had  tariffs slapped on australian goods  bureaucratic obstacles put in the face  of australian exports  i think if in those circumstances the uk  had not been able to negotiate a trade  deal with australia  and then i think it would have weakened  western solidarity against china  i also think there's been a very  important demonstration here because uh  new zealand has been very very cautious  about criticizing china it doesn't want  to go through what australia has gone  through indeed the new zealand has  suggested that the answer to the  australia china problem is for australia  to show more respect to beijing  and but i think there is a hope there is  i mean there's a reason to hope here  that if you can say to me look you have  got yourself into this position of  dependency on china which is frankly  unhealthy  there are alternative trading options  available i i think that would be a good  thing i think i think that you know  australia has woken up  to the dangers created by it by by the  fact that a  economic relationship with china china  will try and use that to  influence your foreign policy and i  think we have to hope that new zealand  will realize the dangers of this too  and just very briefly the other point  that needs to be made is that a lot of  countries are  are climate hypocrites frankly um  australia is often criticized  because we don't want to sign up  instantly  to some commitment 30 years hence  but if you actually look at what we have  achieved  our emissions are down almost 15 percent  since 2005 uh canada  which is a climate darling its emissions  are down  just one percent and new zealand which  likewise likes to parade its climate  virtue  its emissions are down only four percent  so  look at what countries do uh not  at what they promise to do and when it  comes to  actually getting things done australia  is pretty damn good  james do you think the uk government can  learn from australia on climate change  i think there is an interesting question  about i think ambitious targets do  serve a purpose uh in that uh  they they force politicians to confront  hard choices that they might  otherwise avoid but but i think that  your these obviously these targets  don't work if countries don't meet them  and and the other thing that has to be  considered is the cost of meeting these  targets now it's all very well for  politicians to say look in 30 years time  we'll do this  but they won't be held accountable for  the costs of actually meeting those  targets their successes  two or three generations political  generations later  we'll we'll have to do all of this and  while all of us want  uh to get emissions down as far and as  fast  as we can there is a very significant  cost to all of this  in terms of jobs in more traditional  industries  in terms of the extra cost of redundancy  to renewable power  and none of this can be discounted  tony and james thank you very much  [Music]  you

